  "articleBody": "It’s super disheartening to see stains pop up on your beautiful new marble tile, isn't it? Marble is a gorgeous natural stone, but it’s also quite porous, which makes it more susceptible to staining if not handled correctly during installation and afterward. One big reason for staining can be using the wrong type of thin-set, especially for lighter-colored marble. A white, non-staining thin-set is usually recommended to prevent discoloration from bleeding through the stone. Another major factor is improper or insufficient sealing. Marble needs careful sealing before and often after grouting, because grout itself can contain pigments or liquids that can penetrate unsealed stone. And speaking of grout, using sanded grout for narrow joints on polished marble can cause tiny scratches, creating areas where dirt and spills can get trapped and appear as stains. Plus, if the grout isn't sealed, it's just another pathway for liquids to get into your marble. Skipping steps like these, or not allowing proper cure times, can lead to these frustrating issues. This is where professional installation truly shines; an experienced installer knows the specific needs of marble to ensure its lasting beauty. If you're seeing stains, it might be a sign that those crucial steps were missed, and it's definitely worth having a professional take a look.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
